{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And she went down.</b> From her palace." "<b>To wash.</b> It was the custom of Egyptian women, and Scripture mentions her maidens who walk along the place of the river, like \"and you shall have a tool\" (Deuteronomy 23:14), because she sent her maiden, and she is one of the maidens, to take the ark, for the king's daughter would not enter the place of the reeds, for in a place far from dry land the ark was placed, so that no passerby would reach it, and furthermore because it is not grammatical for it to be זרועה, for the mem is weak, and the mem of אמתה (her maiden) below (Exodus 2:5?) has a dagesh, and furthermore because אמתה (her forearm) is a measure, not something spread out."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {1 "The internal citation '(למטה ל, ב)' likely refers to the same verse, Exodus 2:5, where אמתה appears with a dagesh in the mem; 'למטה' (below) may mean later in the same passage or in the section that follows. The wordplay between אמתה 'her maiden' and אמתה 'her forearm' is central to the grammatical argument."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:59.803352071Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}